fe7b4292-2819-4c55-a667-bd9523901a5b	b201a860-05a3-4ddc-bb89-4c4271177271	protocol_analysis	[{"role": "user", "content": "Analyze my ALT (SGPT) data and provide root cause insights. What in my protocol might be affecting this marker?", "timestamp": "2025-12-30T23:34:12.319Z"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "## Pattern Recognition\\n\\nYour ALT has shown a concerning upward trend, rising 54% from baseline (35 → 54 IU/L) and now exceeding optimal range. The progression shows acceleration: relatively stable through mid-2024 (34-44 range), then a notable jump to 54 by December 2025. \\n\\n**Critical context**: Your AST remains normal at 32 IU/L (from August 2024), creating an ALT-predominant elevation pattern that suggests hepatocellular stress rather than muscle damage.\\n\\n## Most Likely Contributors (Ranked)\\n\\n1. **Supplement/Medication Load [HIGHEST PROBABILITY]** — ALT is highly sensitive to hepatic processing burden. The accelerating trend since mid-2024 suggests something was added or increased around that timeframe. Common culprits include high-dose vitamins (especially fat-soluble), herbal supplements, or any new additions to your protocol.\\n\\n2. **Metabolic Stress from Body Composition Changes** — If you've been in a prolonged caloric deficit, doing intensive training, or following ketogenic protocols, this can elevate ALT through increased hepatic gluconeogenesis and fat oxidation demands.\\n\\n3. **Cumulative Oxidative Load** — Your multiple daily devices (LLLT, LED therapy, microneedling) create hormetic stress. While generally beneficial, excessive frequency without adequate recovery could contribute to systemic oxidative burden.\\n\\n## Protective Factors\\n\\n- **Normal alkaline phosphatase (69 IU/L)** — Rules out biliary obstruction\\n- **Excellent albumin (4.7 g/dL)** — Liver synthetic function intact\\n- **Normal AST:ALT ratio** — Suggests hepatocellular rather than alcoholic pattern\\n- **Stable bilirubin (1.2 mg/dL)** — No significant hepatic dysfunction\\n\\n## Recommendations\\n\\n**Investigate:**\\n- Complete your missing liver panel (AST, GGT) from December 2025 testing\\n- Detailed supplement audit: What was added/increased since mid-2024?\\n- Review any medications, including OTC and \\"natural\\" products\\n- Consider comprehensive metabolic panel including glucose, insulin\\n\\n**Adjust:**\\n- Implement 2-week supplement \\"washout\\" of non-essential items\\n- If on intensive training/diet protocol, consider refeed/deload period\\n- Reduce device frequency temporarily (LLLT every other day, LED 2x/week max)\\n\\n**Monitor:**\\n- Retest full hepatic panel in 4-6 weeks\\n- Track correlation with any protocol changes\\n- Watch for symptoms: fatigue, abdominal discomfort, changes in urine/stool\\n\\n## Connect to Goals\\n\\nThis elevation, while mild, represents a 54% increase from your baseline—your liver is signaling stress. Since optimization requires sustainable protocols, identifying and addressing this hepatic burden is crucial for long-term performance and longevity goals. The good news: ALT typically responds quickly to intervention when caught early.\\n\\n**Priority action**: Supplement audit and temporary protocol simplification while monitoring response.", "timestamp": "2025-12-30T23:34:12.319Z"}]	{"biomarkerName": "ALT (SGPT)", "recentChanges": 0, "activeSupplements": 0, "relevantInteractions": 0}	2025-12-30 23:34:12.319+00	2025-12-30 23:34:12.319+00	\N	\N
