bd2c61f6-5525-48a3-be01-18b7251874f4	b201a860-05a3-4ddc-bb89-4c4271177271	biomarker_analysis	[{"role": "user", "content": "Analyze ALT (SGPT): 54 IU/L", "timestamp": "2025-12-30T22:26:23.266Z"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Your ALT level at 54 IU/L represents a mild elevation above the optimal range, and the 58.8% increase is worth attention. Looking at your history, your levels were consistently in the healthy range (29-44 IU/L) until this recent jump to 54. This suggests something has changed recently that's affecting your liver function.\\n\\nALT is primarily found in liver cells, so elevated levels typically indicate liver stress or mild inflammation. The good news is that 54 IU/L is only moderately elevated - not in the range that suggests serious liver damage. However, the upward trend from your previously stable levels warrants investigation.\\n\\nConsider what might have changed in recent months: new medications, supplements, increased alcohol consumption, weight gain, or dietary changes. Even common over-the-counter medications like acetaminophen can elevate ALT if used frequently. Fatty liver disease, often linked to metabolic changes, is another common cause of mild ALT elevation.\\n\\nI'd recommend scheduling a follow-up test in 4-6 weeks to see if this is a temporary spike or a continuing trend. Meanwhile, focus on liver-friendly habits: limit alcohol, maintain a healthy weight, stay hydrated, and review any medications or supplements with your healthcare provider. If the elevation persists or increases further, your doctor may want to investigate underlying causes more thoroughly.", "timestamp": "2025-12-30T22:26:23.266Z"}]	\N	2025-12-30 22:26:23.266+00	2025-12-30 22:26:23.266+00	\N	\N
