ing itself creates cooler temperatures through thick stone walls. Combined with the riverside promenade just steps away, Pinhão station anchors a walkable zone that can occupy families for hours."}, "interesting_facts": ["The approximately 3,047 tiles were hand-painted using techniques unchanged since the 17th century", "Blue color comes from cobalt oxide—the same compound that colors glass blue", "The station is still fully operational—you can take a train from here", "Some panels show the Cachão da Valeira rapids that were destroyed by dynamite in 1780 to improve navigation", "Workers in crushing panels wear 'zapatas'—wooden sandals to protect their feet during hours of grape-treading"]}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
