821a776c-cf3d-45c7-90ff-c78d748dc97f	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	51ed9b75-a060-4d60-a7d8-e49d6002d4c9	São Bento Railway Station	Gentle first activity—free, air-conditioned, visually stunning.	activity	\N	04:30:00	\N	São Bento Station	Praça de Almeida Garrett, 4000-069 Porto	41.1454920	-8.6095440	https://www.google.com/maps/place/?q=place_id:ChIJR_lvdeRkJA0RPneoSR6STxI	Free, air-conditioned, visually stunning, and tells Portuguese history through 20,000 hand-painted azulejo tiles—perfect for kids who can spot knights, ships, battles, and weddings.	\N	\N	\N	USD	\N	\N	f	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	4	\N	2026-01-26 00:33:40.108265+00	2026-01-26 04:29:46.234742+00	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	unconfirmed	\N	\N	58d8b76c-7574-4c4f-964c-b946fcfaa565	ChIJR_lvdeRkJA0RPneoSR6STxI	4.7	4711	\N	\N	t	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["Parker, these pictures tell real stories from almost 1,000 years ago! Can you find the battle scene where Portuguese soldiers are fighting? Look for the horses and swords.", "Count how many different blue colors you can see—the artist had to mix each shade differently!"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["Xander, look UP! Do you see the horsies? Neigh neigh!", "Can you find a boat? There's a big boat somewhere on the wall!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["Charlotte, this is the biggest picture book ever made—but it's all on the walls! Can you find the horses? There are SO many horses!", "Look for the princess in the pretty dress—she's going to marry the king!"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"the_story": {"content": "Jorge Colaço (1868-1942) was already Portugal's most celebrated tile painter when he received the São Bento commission. He had studied in Madrid and Paris, absorbing influences from Art Nouveau and academic painting, but his heart belonged to azulejos. For São Bento, Colaço chose scenes that would speak to regional and national pride: the 1140 Battle of Valdevez, the entry of King João I and Philippa of Lancaster into Porto for their 1387 wedding, the 1415 Conquest of Ceuta, and the arrival of Egas Moniz at the court of Alfonso VII."}, "what_it_is": "A working railway station whose vestibule is covered in over 20,000 hand-painted blue-and-white azulejo tiles depicting pivotal moments in Portuguese history.", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Main Vestibule - Left Wall", "highlights": ["Battle of Valdevez (1140)", "King João I and Philippa wedding procession (1387)"]}, {"area": "Main Vestibule - Right Wall", "highlights": ["Conquest of Ceuta (1415) - Prince Henry visible", "Rural life scenes"]}, {"area": "Upper Frieze", "highlights": ["Evolution of transportation: horses, carriages, boats, trains"]}], "why_it_matters": {"content": "São Bento represents the pinnacle of azulejo art—the distinctive Portuguese ceramic tile tradition that evolved from Moorish influences into a uniquely national art form. Artist Jorge Colaço spent eleven years (1905-1916) creating these panels, working from historical paintings and documents to ensure accuracy in details of armor, costume, and setting. The station itself was built on the site of a former Benedictine monastery (São Bento, hence the name), demolished in the late 19th century to make way for modern transportation."}, "interesting_facts": ["Colaço used over 20,000 individual tiles", "Project took 11 years (1905-1916)", "Station sits on former Benedictine monastery site"]}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-01-26 04:29:46.134+00	\N	\N	\N	\N
9c903a03-3123-45cb-ad12-6e8f790f0674	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	a5653997-917a-4a85-86d7-390497d9457e	Breakfast at hotel	Full breakfast before cork farm visit.	restaurant	\N	07:30:00	\N	Convento do Espinheiro	Canaviais, 7000-502 Évora	\N	\N	https://maps.google.com/?q=38.6008,-7.8883	Maximum loyalty benefits with Marriott Gold status. Exceptional pools (essential). Historic 15th-century monastery with 5th night free on points. Daily complimentary activities for families.	\N	\N	\N	USD	\N	\N	f	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	0	\N	2026-02-06 17:48:29.987079+00	2026-02-06 17:48:29.987079+00	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	unconfirmed	\N	\N	909968ea-bcec-49ea-b7a8-fffcd9249552	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["Parker, we're staying in a real castle-monastery where actual Portuguese kings lived 500 years ago!", "King João II made important decisions here about explorers sailing around the world.", "Tomorrow at the hotel tour, you can ask about secret passages and look for the oldest stone in the building.", "The monks who lived here took vows of silence—can you imagine not talking for a whole day?"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["Xander, big pool! See the big trees? Those trees are OLD—older than Mommy and Daddy!", "We sleep in a castle with big, big doors!", "Splash splash in the pool!", "Look at all the grass to run on! Run run run!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["Charlotte, our hotel used to be home to monks who wore long brown robes and sang songs every day!", "But guess what? REAL princesses also stayed here!", "There are TWO pools—one where you can splash and one where the grown-ups swim.", "We'll have dinner in a room with a curved ceiling that looks like a cave!"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"the_story": {"content": "The monastery was founded around 1400 after a shepherd reported a vision of the Virgin Mary above a thorn bush (espinheiro means 'thorn bush'). The site was consecrated in 1458 for the Hieronymite Order, the same order that built Lisbon's Jerónimos Monastery.\\n\\nPortuguese royalty regularly visited and stayed at the Convento do Espinheiro. Kings João II, Manuel I, Afonso V, and Sebastião all slept within these walls. The church contains one of Portugal's most prestigious noble family pantheons—generations of Portuguese aristocracy are interred here.\\n\\nA three-year restoration transformed the monastery into a luxury hotel while preserving original frescoes, Gothic architecture, and sacred spaces. The conversion maintained the cloister, chapel, and monastic atmosphere while adding modern comforts. Today's guests walk the same corridors as medieval monks and Renaissance kings."}, "what_it_is": "A meticulously restored 15th-century monastery converted into a 5-star luxury hotel, part of Marriott's Luxury Collection, featuring 92 rooms across 8 hectares of gardens with multiple pools and daily complimentary activities.", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Historic Monastery", "highlights": ["15th-century church", "Gothic cloisters", "Original frescoes", "Noble family pantheon"]}, {"area": "Pool Complex", "highlights": ["Outdoor infinity pool", "Two children's pools", "17-meter indoor heated pool", "Poolside service"]}, {"area": "Gardens", "highlights": ["8 hectares", "Ancient olive groves", "Tennis court", "Children's playground"]}, {"area": "Dining", "highlights": ["Divinus Restaurant in Gothic wine cellar", "Olive Restaurant for family-friendly Italian", "Poolside service"]}], "why_it_matters": {"content": "The Convento do Espinheiro represents the optimal balance of luxury experience, loyalty benefits, and family functionality for your Alentejo stay. As a Marriott Luxury Collection property, your Gold Elite status provides meaningful benefits: 25% bonus points, room upgrades when available, and 2:00 PM late checkout.\\n\\nThe property addresses every family accommodation criterion. Pools are essential in Alentejo's June heat—the hotel delivers with outdoor infinity pool, two children's pools, AND a 17-meter indoor heated pool for backup. The 8 hectares of gardens provide space for children to run. Connecting rooms accommodate your family of 5. And the 5th Night Free benefit on points bookings makes the 5-night stay 20% more efficient.\\n\\nBeyond logistics, the hotel offers an experience unavailable elsewhere: sleeping in a 15th-century monastery where Portuguese kings once stayed, dining in a Gothic wine cellar, and participating in daily complimentary activities including wine tasting and historical tours."}, "interesting_facts": ["Founded after shepherd's vision of Virgin Mary above thorn bush (1400)", "Kings João II, Manuel I, Afonso V, and Sebastião all stayed here", "Three-year restoration preserved original frescoes", "8 hectares of gardens with ancient olive trees", "Daily complimentary wine tasting in Gothic cistern", "Children's playground on property"]}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
a4f7b56a-0aa6-4aa5-93fa-e5f25daa1bc3	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	a5653997-917a-4a85-86d7-390497d9457e	Drive to cork farm	30 minutes to Portugal Farm Experience.	transport	\N	08:30:00	\N	Route to Montemor-o-Novo	Montemor-o-Novo (30 min from Évora)	\N	\N	Contact for directions	Portugal produces 50% of world's cork. June dates fall during harvest season—may witness actual cork stripping. Hands-on experience perfect for homeschool education. Children 0-9 are FREE.	\N	\N	\N	USD	\N	\N	f	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	1	\N	2026-02-06 17:48:29.987079+00	2026-02-06 17:48:29.987079+00	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	unconfirmed	\N	\N	909968ea-bcec-49ea-b7a8-fffcd9249552	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["Parker, cork is one of the coolest materials on Earth. It's waterproof, fireproof, and bounces!", "NASA uses it to protect spacecraft from burning up when they return to Earth. And we've been harvesting it the SAME WAY for thousands of years.", "No machines—just skilled workers with axes. Watch how carefully they cut—one wrong move and they hurt the tree. It's like surgery!", "See those numbers painted on the trees? Can you figure out what year each tree was last harvested?", "If a tree says '7', when was it harvested? And when will it be ready again? That's a 9-year math problem!"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["Xander, touch the tree! Feel squishy? Squishy squishy!", "Now knock on it—knock knock! Does it make a sound?", "Look at the number on the tree—can you see the number?", "Let's find the piggy! What sound does a pig make? Oink oink!", "Can you find a big stick? A little stick? Let's collect sticks!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["Charlotte, feel this bark—is it hard or squishy? Cork trees wear a special coat made of this squishy stuff!", "When people take off the coat, the tree makes a NEW one all by itself! See that red color? That's like the tree's skin showing.", "In 9 years, it'll grow a whole new thick coat. Let's count to nine—that's how many years the tree needs!", "Can you find a number painted on a tree? What number is it?", "And look—there's a pig! These piggies eat acorns that fall from the trees!"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"the_story": {"content": "Cork oak (Quercus suber) evolved alongside the Alentejo landscape over millennia. The Romans recognized cork's unique properties—waterproof, buoyant, fire-resistant—using it for fishing floats and wine vessel stoppers. The word 'cork' may derive from the Arabic 'quorq,' reflecting the Moorish period's influence on Iberian cork culture.\\n\\nCork harvesting is remarkably sustainable. Trees must reach 25 years old before their first harvest, and the initial 'virgin cork' is too coarse for wine stoppers. Only the third harvest (at age 43+) produces quality cork. Trees can be harvested every 9 years and live 200-300 years, potentially providing 20+ harvests per tree.\\n\\nThe harvest season runs mid-June to mid-August, when the cork naturally separates from the trunk. Skilled workers called tiradores use curved-blade axes to cut and pry off the bark without damaging the tree. The year of harvest is painted on each trunk—if you see '7,' that tree was harvested in 2017 and will be ready again in 2026.\\n\\nToday, NASA uses cork to protect spacecraft from burning up during atmospheric reentry. The same material that floated Roman fishing nets now shields astronauts returning to Earth—a testament to cork's extraordinary properties."}, "what_it_is": "A hands-on 2-hour tour of a family-owned cork farm (180+ years of family operation), including forest walks, cork production process, farm animals, and traditional food/wine tasting.", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Cork Forest Walk", "highlights": ["Trees with painted harvest numbers", "Different stages of cork regeneration", "Cork oak ecosystem (birds, insects, wildflowers)"]}, {"area": "Harvest Demonstration (June)", "highlights": ["Skilled tiradores stripping bark", "Traditional tools and techniques", "Exposed red 'skin' of freshly harvested trees"]}, {"area": "Farm Animals", "highlights": ["Pigs (fed on acorns)", "Farm dog Max", "Traditional Alentejo livestock"]}, {"area": "Tasting Session", "highlights": ["Regional wines", "Local cheeses", "Olives and olive oil", "Traditional bread"]}], "why_it_matters": {"content": "Portugal produces over 50% of the world's cork—an industry employing 100,000+ people and worth €1.1 billion annually. Cork harvesting is an ancient art passed through generations, with workers (average age 65) still using the same techniques as centuries ago. Your June 19-24 dates fall perfectly within harvest season—you may witness actual cork stripping if you request it when booking.\\n\\nUnlike most trees, cork oaks survive bark removal because the cork grows outside the cambium layer. The 9-year harvest cycle (marked by a painted number on each tree) has continued unchanged since antiquity. Children can see trees marked with different numbers, calculate when each was last harvested, and understand sustainable resource management.\\n\\nThe farm has been in the same family for 180+ years, representing the continuity that defines Alentejo culture. The experience includes meeting farm animals and tasting regional products—making it accessible and memorable for all ages."}, "interesting_facts": ["Portugal produces 50% of the world's cork", "Cork harvest happens only every 9 years per tree", "Trees must be 25 years old before first harvest", "Cork oaks can live 200-300 years with 20+ harvests", "NASA uses cork to protect spacecraft from heat", "The harvest year is painted on each tree trunk", "June 19-24 falls within harvest season (mid-June to mid-August)"]}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
