fd87e806-f38a-4ce4-84c0-cb1a9a88a445	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	271b6b44-4c9e-44b1-bd46-4f895f7fb950	Final Pastéis de Belém (optional)	Buy a box to go!	restaurant	\N	11:30:00	\N	Pastéis de Belém	Rua de Belém 84-92, 1300-085 Lisboa	38.6975000	-9.2031000	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	USD	\N	\N	f	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	4	\N	2026-02-06 17:49:05.25817+00	2026-02-06 17:49:05.25817+00	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	unconfirmed	\N	\N	ae7c1aaf-2a96-485b-9fa6-afe1724a55fb	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["Parker, only 3-6 people in the WORLD know the secret recipe! They sign contracts and take oaths!", "Parker, these were invented by monks at the monastery we're visiting."], "activities": ["Watch bakers", "Sprinkle own toppings"]}, "xander": {"scripts": ["Xander, YUMMY pastry! Hot! Careful!"], "activities": ["Eat warm pastry", "Attempt sprinkling (messy!)"], "carrier_needed": false}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["Charlotte, you get to sprinkle your OWN cinnamon and sugar!", "Charlotte, look at the shiny brown top—from a super-hot oven!"], "activities": ["Sprinkle own toppings—empowering!"]}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"the_story": "Catholic monks at Jerónimos used massive quantities of egg whites for starching habits, leaving mountains of yolks. When the 1820 Liberal Revolution expelled religious orders, someone began selling the tarts. In 1837, the shop officially opened.", "what_it_is": "World's original pastel de nata bakery, operating since 1837 using secret Jerónimos Monastery recipe.", "why_it_matters": "This is the source. Every pastel de nata worldwide descends from recipes trying to imitate what these monks created. Only 3-6 people know the secret recipe, kept in a vault.", "interesting_facts": ["Secret recipe in vault, known by 3-6 people", "~20,000 tarts baked daily", "Same building since 1837"]}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
831351a9-6919-488e-8db8-4f24c0fd265b	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	6460b177-0192-46b1-9d04-dd2016cbd8d9	Depart Alentejo	2-2.5 hour drive to Lagos	transport	\N	10:00:00	\N	\N	Lagos Marina, Gate 'Passeios de Barco'	\N	\N	https://maps.google.com/?q=37.0971,-8.6703	\N	\N	\N	\N	USD	\N	\N	f	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	0	\N	2026-02-06 17:40:06.645839+00	2026-02-06 17:40:06.645839+00	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	unconfirmed	\N	\N	4f5d2d2f-c4d2-4427-95f4-1e5facc954da	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["These cliffs are 20 million years old—that's before dinosaurs went extinct! The waves carved out these caves, and now we get to be explorers going inside.", "Watch for the rock that looks exactly like an elephant drinking water. See if you can figure out why they called that cave 'The Cathedral.'", "The turquoise color happens because the sand on the bottom is so white it reflects the sunlight—just like a swimming pool but natural!"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["Boat! We're going in the BOAT! Look at the blue water!", "Can you splash your hand in the water? Feel how cool it is!", "We're going through a tunnel—here it comes—WHOOOSH!", "Do you see the fish swimming? Look down in the water!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["We're going into secret mermaid caves! The water is going to glow like magic—it turns bright blue and green because the sun shines through the rocks.", "Look for the rocks shaped like animals—there's a dragon, an elephant, and maybe you'll spot one I haven't seen!", "Can you be the first to spot when the cave opens up into a big room? It's like a castle inside!"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"the_story": "The name 'Ponta da Piedade' means 'Point of Mercy.' Local fishing families named it centuries ago—wives and mothers would gather on these cliffs during storms, praying for mercy (piedade) for their men at sea below. The limestone formations are 20 million years old, shaped by the relentless Atlantic into arches, pinnacles, and secret grottos that only small boats can access. You'll pass through caves nicknamed 'The Cathedral' (dome-shaped), 'The Kitchen,' and 'The Living Room,' while guides point out rock formations resembling elephants, skulls, and intertwined lovers.", "what_it_is": "Small boats (10-12 passengers) thread through narrow sea arches and enter caves carved over millennia into golden limestone cliffs. The water glows emerald and turquoise as light filters through rock formations.", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Sea Caves", "highlights": ["Cathedral Grotto (dome-shaped)", "Kitchen Cave", "Living Room Cave"]}, {"area": "Rock Formations", "highlights": ["Elephant Rock", "Lovers embracing", "Skull formation"]}, {"area": "Water Effects", "highlights": ["Turquoise water glowing in caves", "Light beams through rock openings"]}], "why_it_matters": "This is the Algarve's signature experience—seeing these cliffs from sea level reveals a hidden world invisible from above. For families, it's the rare adventure that works for all ages: short enough for a 3-year-old's attention span, visually spectacular enough for a 5-year-old's imagination, and geologically fascinating for an 8-year-old who loves science.", "interesting_facts": ["The limestone is 20 million years old—predating most dinosaur extinctions", "Small boats can access caves that larger tourist vessels cannot reach", "The turquoise color comes from sunlight reflecting off white sandy bottom", "Fishing families still use these waters—you may see traditional boats"]}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
