70f1df43-2ae1-4228-8499-ab18b2da21c0	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	1b254c40-464c-4598-9cae-1e3b088bcf21	Quinta do Bomfim visit	Vineyard walk, terrace views. Walkable from Pinhão. Grape juice for kids.	activity	\N	04:30:00	\N	Quinta do Bomfim	Pinhão	\N	\N	https://maps.google.com/?q=41.189245,-7.540943	Symington's flagship visitor experience combines spectacular river-view terraces with family-appropriate visit duration. Walkable from Pinhão, outdoor terraces let children watch boats while adults taste, and vineyard walks offer independent exploration.	\N	\N	\N	USD	\N	\N	f	\N	f	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	5	\N	2026-02-06 17:36:45.828049+00	2026-02-06 17:36:45.828049+00	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	unconfirmed	\N	\N	a913d847-c0ea-472a-881e-0e7f43ee2cc4	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	f	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["This family started making wine here 130 years ago—that's before cars, airplanes, or even most electricity. Their great-great-grandparents planted vines that are STILL growing. Can you find the oldest-looking vines?", "See how the stone walls make flat 'steps' on the steep hill? Each wall has to hold back thousands of pounds of dirt. What do you think happens when a wall falls down?", "The boats down on the river used to carry wine from THIS quinta to Porto. Count how many boats you can see from the terrace!"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["Can you find a GRAPE? It's GREEN and round and little! Don't eat it—it's still growing!", "Let's look for rocks! This wall has LOTS of rocks. Can you touch one? Is it HOT or COLD?", "Do you see the RIVER down there? Can you wave to the boats? WAVE WAVE WAVE!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["Let's go on a TREASURE HUNT in the vineyard! Can you find: a tiny baby grape, a really twisty vine, and a lizard on a rock? (Lizards love these warm walls!)", "The hat they gave us is to keep the sun off our heads. Feel how hot the stones are—the grapes cook in the sun all day! That's what makes them SO sweet!", "Look at all the rows of grapes—they're like little green soldiers standing in lines. Why do you think they plant them in rows? [So people can walk between them!]"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"the_story": {"content": "The Symington family story spans continents and centuries. Andrew James Symington arrived in Portugal in 1882 from Scotland, initially working in the textile trade before recognizing opportunity in Port wine. His descendants married into established Portuguese wine families, gradually acquiring quintas and building what became the Douro's largest family-owned Port wine company.\\n\\nQuinta do Bomfim was acquired in 1896 and remains one of the family's flagship properties. The name 'Bomfim' (Portuguese for 'good end') reflects the estate's position: arriving here by river meant the most difficult upstream navigation was complete. The quinta's wines contribute to Dow's Vintage Ports, considered among the finest in the region.\\n\\nWhat makes Bomfim special beyond its wines is the setting. The property occupies a sweeping bend in the Douro with terraces climbing dramatically from water level. The estate house, a 19th-century manor, overlooks formal gardens that contrast with the rugged agricultural landscape beyond. Recent investments have added modern visitor facilities without compromising the historic character.\\n\\nThe Symingtons represent an interesting Douro phenomenon: British families who became effectively Portuguese over generations while maintaining English names and international connections. Their visitor experience reflects this dual identity—English spoken fluently by staff, but the wines and traditions presented are thoroughly Portuguese."}, "what_it_is": "A historic Port wine estate owned by the Symington family (producers of Dow's, Graham's, Warre's, and Cockburn's) offering tastings, vineyard walks, and terrace picnics with sweeping views over the Douro River.", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Visitor Center & Tasting Room", "highlights": ["Historic photos of estate and family", "Tasting room with river views", "Small museum exhibits on Port production"]}, {"area": "Vineyard Walk Route", "highlights": ["80+ year old vine blocks", "Traditional stone terrace walls", "QR-code audio points explaining viticulture", "Panoramic viewpoints over Pinhão"]}, {"area": "Terrace Areas", "highlights": ["River-view seating", "Formal gardens near estate house", "Picnic spots with vineyard backdrop"]}], "why_it_matters": {"content": "Quinta do Bomfim represents the best of family-friendly quinta visiting: genuinely spectacular views, flexible visit formats, and the practical advantage of walkability from Pinhão (10 minutes from the train station). Unlike remote quintas requiring drives on narrow mountain roads, Bomfim sits adjacent to town.\\n\\nThe Symington family has produced Port wine for five generations, making them among the most established British-descended wine families in the Douro. Their approach to tourism reflects this heritage: professional, educational, but without the stuffiness that sometimes accompanies wine estates. The outdoor terrace where tastings often conclude overlooks the river and surrounding quintas—children can watch boats while adults finish their Port.\\n\\nThe vineyard walk option lets families explore terraces independently. For €2 extra, you receive a lightweight sun hat, map, water bottle, and wine glass to carry. Marked paths wind through the estate's oldest vines (some over 80 years old) with QR codes triggering audio commentary. This self-guided format works beautifully for families: move at your own pace, stop when children want to examine something, and complete the circuit in 30-60 minutes depending on interest.\\n\\nFor a special experience, the terrace picnic (€40/person, pre-order 24 hours ahead) provides Portuguese foods, Altano white wine, and Dow's Port on a blanket overlooking the river. It's more expensive than packing your own lunch but eliminates logistics and adds local products: regional cheeses, charcuterie, olive oil, estate-produced preserves."}, "interesting_facts": ["The Symington family owns more quintas than any other producer in the Douro", "Some vines on the estate were planted before 1900 and still produce grapes", "Dow's Port was served at Queen Elizabeth II's coronation in 1953", "The quinta's terraces were hand-built by workers who camped on-site for months during construction"]}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N
