or two centuries. For kids, they look like fairy houses. The flat granite threshing floor offers safe, open space for exploration. The communal nature tells a story of village cooperation."}, "interesting_facts": ["The oldest espigueiro predates the French Revolution", "A single espigueiro could store 500-1000kg of grain", "Classified as National Monument since 1938"]}	\N	{"cost": "Free", "hours": "Always accessible (outdoor)", "duration": "30-45 min espigueiros; half-day with lunch and swimming", "reservation_required": false}	{"lat": 41.8711, "lng": -8.2594, "name": "Espigueiros de Soajo", "address": "Largo do Eiró, Soajo", "google_maps_url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=41.8711,-8.2594"}	\N	\N
0d7f4453-2f14-40b3-9f6c-d999f20f31f3	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	8e5d2c22-331b-4399-90b6-574bac6205a8	attraction	Traditional Villages	Lindoso Castle & Espigueiros	\N	"Medieval castle Parker can actually explore paired with Portugal's largest espigueiro collection—double the historic impact with added fortress adventure."	\N	Claude Research Agent	2026-02-06	\N	\N	Lindoso, Ponte da Barca	\N	\N	https://maps.google.com/?q=41.8689,-8.2039	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Castle exterior always; interior typically 10am-6pm	\N	\N	\N	EUR	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	approved	must_do	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["This castle stopped the Spanish army from invading! See how the walls are designed for defense?", "Look through that arrow slit—archers would stand here during battles!"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["CASTLE! Big castle!", "Look—a window! Wave out the window!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["A REAL castle! Knights really lived here!", "Can you see the little houses down below?"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-02-06 17:40:43.863395+00	2026-02-06 17:40:43.863395+00	{"the_story": {"content": "The castle guards one of the few mountain passes into Portugal from Spain. Built primarily under King Dinis I (1279-1325), it saw action in Portuguese-Castilian wars and was damaged during the Peninsular War."}, "what_it_is": "A 13th-century border fortress overlooking Portugal's largest espigueiro collection (50+ granaries).", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Castle", "highlights": ["13th-century walls", "Square keep", "Arrow slits", "Mountain views from battlements"]}, {"area": "Espigueiro Terraces", "highlights": ["50+ granite granaries", "Communal threshing floors", "Castle-above-espigueiros views"]}], "why_it_matters": {"content": "While Soajo has prettier espigueiros, Lindoso has a CASTLE. For an eight-year-old, that changes everything. Parker can stand on actual medieval battlements, look through arrow slits, and imagine defending against Spanish invaders."}, "interesting_facts": ["The castle was briefly abandoned in the 18th century", "This is Portugal's largest espigueiro collection", "Some damage from French cannonballs still visible"]}	\N	{"cost": "Exterior free; interior €1.50", "hours": "Castle exterior always; interior typically 10am-6pm", "duration": "1-1.5 hours", "reservation_required": false}	{"lat": 41.8689, "lng": -8.2039, "name": "Castelo de Lindoso", "address": "Lindoso, Ponte da Barca", "google_maps_url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=41.8689,-8.2039"}	\N	\N
df7c1ef2-1b4c-46ac-9aeb-9bffe04bb1a5	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	8e5d2c22-331b-4399-90b6-574bac6205a8	activity	Adventure Activities	Horseback Riding with Gerês Rails	\N	"The ONLY operator with NO minimum age—Xander can ride with a parent while older children have guides walking alongside."	\N	Claude Research Agent	2026-02-06	\N	\N	Campo do Gerês area	\N	\N	https://maps.google.com/?q=41.7289,-8.1542	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	By appointment; morning rides recommended	\N	\N	\N	EUR	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	approved	must_do	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["See how the horse moves its ears? Forward means interested, back means listening to you!", "Keep your heels down and toes up—that's how real riders balance!"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["HORSE! Big horse! Pat the neck gently!", "Clip clop, clip clop!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["Can you feel the horse's heartbeat?", "The horse's name is [name]. Can you say hello?"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-02-06 17:40:43.863395+00	2026-02-06 17:40:43.863395+00	{"the_story": {"content": "Gerês Rails maintains horses selected for temperament rather than speed. Their guides grew up with horses as transportation and farm tools. Trail routes utilize ancient pathways connecting mountain villages."}, "what_it_is": "Guided horseback riding through mountain trails with a local company specializing in families with young children.", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Riding Trails", "highlights": ["Mountain panoramas", "Forest sections", "Traditional landscapes", "Potential wild Garrano sightings"]}], "why_it_matters": {"content": "Most operators set minimum ages at 5-6 years. Gerês Rails lets Xander ride on the same horse as a parent. Guides walk alongside, leading horses at walking speed. €35/person makes this accessible for a family of five."}, "interesting_facts": ["Some trails follow routes used for transhumance migration", "Portuguese mountain horses developed sure-footedness over centuries", "Traditional horse fairs date to medieval times"]}	\N	{"cost": "€35 per person", "hours": "By appointment; morning rides recommended", "contact": {"email": "geral@geresrails.pt", "phone": "+351 939 592 537"}, "duration": "1.5-2.5 hours including jeep transfer", "booking_url": "https://www.geresrails.pt/passeio-cavalo/", "reservation_required": true}	{"lat": 41.7289, "lng": -8.1542, "name": "Gerês Rails", "address": "Campo do Gerês area", "google_maps_url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=41.7289,-8.1542"}	\N	\N
9e0d9ed2-4cd8-4529-930d-177e969c086a	2e2ae20a-832b-4e7c-9419-2afdb506b6ab	58d8b76c-7574-4c4f-964c-b946fcfaa565	attraction	Historic Site / Art	São Bento Railway Station	\N	"Free, air-conditioned, visually stunning, and tells Portuguese history through 20,000 hand-painted azulejo tiles—perfect for kids who can spot knights, ships, battles, and weddings."	\N	Claude Research Agent	2026-01-26	\N	\N	Praça de Almeida Garrett, 4000-069 Porto	\N	\N	https://maps.google.com/?q=41.1456,-8.6109	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	24/7 (working train station)	\N	\N	\N	EUR	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	approved	must_do	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	{"parker": {"scripts": ["Parker, these pictures tell real stories from almost 1,000 years ago! Can you find the battle scene where Portuguese soldiers are fighting? Look for the horses and swords.", "Count how many different blue colors you can see—the artist had to mix each shade differently!"], "age_at_trip": 8}, "xander": {"scripts": ["Xander, look UP! Do you see the horsies? Neigh neigh!", "Can you find a boat? There's a big boat somewhere on the wall!"], "age_at_trip": 3}, "charlotte": {"scripts": ["Charlotte, this is the biggest picture book ever made—but it's all on the walls! Can you find the horses? There are SO many horses!", "Look for the princess in the pretty dress—she's going to marry the king!"], "age_at_trip": 5}}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-01-26 00:33:42.564442+00	2026-01-26 00:33:42.564442+00	{"the_story": {"content": "Jorge Colaço (1868-1942) was already Portugal's most celebrated tile painter when he received the São Bento commission. He had studied in Madrid and Paris, absorbing influences from Art Nouveau and academic painting, but his heart belonged to azulejos. For São Bento, Colaço chose scenes that would speak to regional and national pride: the 1140 Battle of Valdevez, the entry of King João I and Philippa of Lancaster into Porto for their 1387 wedding, the 1415 Conquest of Ceuta, and the arrival of Egas Moniz at the court of Alfonso VII."}, "what_it_is": "A working railway station whose vestibule is covered in over 20,000 hand-painted blue-and-white azulejo tiles depicting pivotal moments in Portuguese history.", "what_youll_see": [{"area": "Main Vestibule - Left Wall", "highlights": ["Battle of Valdevez (1140)", "King João I and Philippa wedding procession (1387)"]}, {"area": "Main Vestibule - Right Wall", "highlights": ["Conquest of Ceuta (1415) - Prince Henry visible", "Rural life scenes"]}, {"area": "Upper Frieze", "highlights": ["Evolution of transportation: horses, carriages, boats, trains"]}], "why_it_matters": {"content": "São Bento represents the pinnacle of azulejo art—the distinctive Portuguese ceramic tile tradition that evolved from Moorish influences into a uniquely national art form. Artist Jorge Colaço spent eleven years (1905-1916) creating these panels, working from historical paintings and documents to ensure accuracy in details of armor, costume, and setting. The station itself was built on the site of a former Benedictine monastery (São Bento, hence the name), demolished in the late 19th century to make way for modern transportation."}, "interesting_facts": ["Colaço used over 20,000 individual tiles", "Project took 11 years (1905-1916)", "Station sits on former Benedictine monastery site"]}	\N	{"cost": "Free", "hours": "24/7 (working train station)", "duration": "20-30 minutes", "accessibility": "Fully accessible, air-conditioned", "reservation_required": false}	{"lat": 41.1456, "lng": -8.6109, "name": "Estação de São Bento", "address": "Praça de Almeida Garrett, 4000-069 Porto", "google_maps_url": "https://maps.google.com/?q=41.1456,-8.6109"}	\N	\N
